this is the tungsten pendulum Bob this  was one of the first pieces of metal  that we machined for the clock and this  was made for the first prototype either  as a Huygens style pendulum or even as a  torsional style pendulum in a Horgan's  pendulum is the one you probably  recognized as a grandfather style clock  we ended up using a torsional pendulum  which is a twisting pendulum it has a  hole through the center that that has a  little bump in the center that's  actually specific to a one second  Huygens style pendulum and this  particular material is probably one of  the longest lasting materials that we're  using in the clock both because it's  extremely dense and heavy almost as  dense as gold or platinum would be as  well as the fact that it has one of the  highest melting temperatures of any  metal unknown and so this particular  object would probably last in the  hundreds of thousands or millions of  years and that little bump that's in the  center being specific to this materials  expansion rate of based on temperature  actually makes it specific to one second  so an archaeologist finding this many  millennia into the future might actually  be able to determine our basic unit of  time just from this single object alone  so as I said before this is an extremely  dense object and I have with me the  aluminum version that we used to as a  test piece right before we made this  final tungsten one and you can see how  much of an indent the aluminum makes in  the pillow and this is the tungsten  version
 

